<spell-notebook "advertisement usage">
	<header level="1">
		Advertisement
		<header>An ad displays third-party promotional content.</>
	</>
	<advertisement-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#unit-sizes">Unit sizes</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-ad>` is only the slot: it sizes a box to an IAB unit and lays out whatever you put in it.
			Loading the ad itself is up to your ad network's script.

			- `unit` takes the unit's words (`medium rectangle`, `leaderboard`, `large mobile banner` ...),
			  not `size`, which is `mini` ... `massive` on every component.
			- `test` shows a grey placeholder until the real ad arrives: bare, it says "Ad" (translated);
			  with a value, it says that.
			- `align="center"` centres the box in its container.
			- Mobile units (`mobile leaderboard`, `large mobile banner`) show only on phone-sized viewports:
			  they're served for the device, not for the container's width.
			- It has no events.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<ui-ad unit="medium rectangle" test="Your ad here">
			  <iframe src="https://ads.example.com/unit/123" title="Advertisement" width="300" height="250"></iframe>
			</ui-ad>
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Unit sizes">
		<markdown>
			Unit sizes are the IAB's, in `px`, on purpose: an ad unit is a pixel contract with the ad server,
			not a typographic size, so they aren't tokens. Units wider than the column (`leaderboard` 728 px,
			`billboard` 970 px) overflow it; give them a wide enough region.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			None of its own: the box takes no focus (`delegatesFocus` is off). Links and frames inside the
			ad take focus as usual.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- An ad is a plain block with no role, so several ads on a page don't create duplicate landmarks.
			  Where you want one, wrap the ad column in a named `<aside aria-label="Sponsored">`.
			- The `test` text is drawn by CSS and isn't reliably read aloud; it's a layout placeholder, not
			  content.
			- The ad's own content needs its own text alternative: an `alt` on an image link, a `title` on an
			  `<iframe>`.
			- If the element fails to render, it shows its children through a bare `<slot>`: the ad's own image,
			  link or frame still shows, but the unit loses its fixed size, and a bare `test` shows nothing.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
